    followUp: "Ophthalmology clinic: 4-6 weeks for IOP recheck after adding Timolol, then every 3 months for monitoring. If IOP not controlled on 3 agents: refer for SLT or trabeculectomy. Annual visual fields and OCT. Screen first-degree relatives for glaucoma (2-4x increased risk with family history).",
    pearls: "POAG is the most common type of glaucoma worldwide and the leading cause of irreversible blindness. Risk factors: elevated IOP, age >40, family history, African descent, myopia, thin corneas. The \"30% rule\": target IOP is 30% below untreated baseline IOP. Adherence is the greatest challenge in glaucoma management — once-daily prostaglandin analogues have better adherence than BID/TID regimens. Timolol is contraindicated in asthma and severe COPD due to beta-2 blockade causing bronchospasm. Disc haemorrhage is a sign of disease progression, not just a finding — it precedes visual field loss. In Kenya, glaucoma accounts for approximately 15-20% of blindness cases, and many patients present late with advanced disease.",
    references: ["AAO Guidelines: Primary Open-Angle Glaucoma", "NICE Guidelines: Glaucoma (NG81)", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Ophthalmology"],
