    hpi: "Lydia Obiero, a 81-year-old child from Nandi Hills, presents with a 4-day history of bilateral red eyes with thick, purulent yellow-green discharge. Mother reports the child wakes up with eyelids crusted shut and needs to be cleaned with warm water before opening. The child complains of a \"sandy\" feeling in both eyes but denies pain or difficulty seeing. No history of trauma, foreign body, or contact lens use. Several children at school have had similar red eyes in the past 2 weeks. No fever, no cough, no respiratory symptoms.",
    pmh: "No significant PMH. No known drug allergies. Fully immunised for age.. Also has Hypothyroidism.",
    medHx: "No regular medications. No recent antibiotic use.",
    allergies: "NKDA",
    pe: "Both eyes: bilateral diffuse conjunctival injection with moderate purulent mucopurulent discharge. Eyelid oedema present bilaterally, worse in the mornings. Cornea: clear bilaterally, no fluorescein staining (no epithelial defect or keratitis). No preauricular lymphadenopathy. No chemosis. VA: child cooperates for basic testing — appears normal bilaterally. Pupils: equal, round, reactive.",
    vitals: "HR: 99 bpm, BP: 131/63 mmHg, RR: 23 breaths/min, Temp: 37.8°C, SpO2: 94%",
    labs: "No labs required for straightforward bacterial conjunctivitis. If recurrent or not responding to treatment: conjunctival swab for culture and sensitivity, Gram stain.",
    imaging: "No imaging indicated.",
    diagnosis: "Acute Bacterial Conjunctivitis (Mucopurulent Type) — likely Staphylococcus aureus or Haemophilus influenzae",
    ddx: ["Bacterial Conjunctivitis", "Viral Conjunctivitis (watery discharge, preauricular lymphadenopathy, often unilateral initially)", "Allergic Conjunctivitis (itching, clear/watery discharge, papillae on everting lids)", "Chlamydial Conjunctivitis (chronic, inclusion conjunctivitis)", "Keratoconjunctivitis (corneal involvement — pain, photophobia, reduced VA)"],
    goals: "Eradicate infection within 5-7 days, reduce contagion, prevent complications (keratitis, secondary infection), educate parents on hygiene",
    pharm: "1. Topical antibiotic (first-line): Chloramphenicol 0.5% eye drops — 1-2 drops in affected eye(s) every 2 hours while awake for the first 2 days, then every 4 hours for 5-7 days (total 7-day course). 2. Alternative: Fusidic Acid 1% eye ointment — apply a small strip (约5mm) inside the lower eyelid BID for 7 days (better for young children — less frequent dosing, cream formulation). 3. If severe or not responding: Ciprofloxacin 0.3% drops q2-6h (covers broader spectrum including Pseudomonas). 4. For crusting: clean eyelids gently with warm damp cotton wool before applying drops. 5. No role for oral antibiotics in simple conjunctivitis. 6. Both eyes should be treated even if one appears less affected (infection spreads easily between eyes).",
    nonPharm: "Strict hand hygiene — wash hands with soap before and after touching the child's eyes. Use separate towels, flannels, and pillowcases. Do not share eye drops with other family members. Clean discharge with separate cotton wool for each eye. Keep the child home from school/daycare until 24 hours after starting antibiotic drops. Dispose of old discharge cloths safely. Avoid swimming pools until conjunctivitis resolved.",
    carePlan: "Start Chloramphenicol 0.5% drops (or Fusidic Acid ointment) in both eyes. Educate mother on eyelid cleaning technique and hand hygiene. Counsel on when to return: if no improvement in 48 hours, if pain develops, if photophobia occurs, or if vision becomes blurred. Review at 7 days or sooner if worsening. Check for corneal involvement (fluorescein) if pain or photophobia develops at any point.",
    dtps: "1. Common childhood infectious conjunctivitis in school setting with close contact. 2. Self-limiting but antibiotics shorten duration and reduce transmission. 3. Parent education critical for hygiene compliance and preventing spread.",
    monitoring: "Symptom improvement expected within 48-72 hours of starting antibiotics. If no improvement by 48 hours: review diagnosis, consider culture, or alternative organism (may need fluoroquinolone drops). Check daily for: pain (suggests keratitis), photophobia (suggests anterior uveitis or keratitis), decreased vision (serious — refer urgently). Contact lens wearers (if applicable) require urgent ophthalmology referral to rule out Pseudomonas aeruginosa keratitis.",
    counselling: "The eye drops are safe for your child. Clean the crusty discharge with warm water and cotton wool before putting the drops in. Wash your hands every time before and after touching the eyes. Your child can return to school 24 hours after starting the drops. Use a separate towel and do not share it with other children. If the eyes do not improve in 2 days, or if your child develops pain or sensitivity to light, come back immediately.",
    followUp: "Follow-up at 7 days if no improvement. If recurrent bacterial conjunctivitis (>3 episodes in 6 months): consider nasolacrimal duct obstruction (especially in younger children — may need probing), chronic blepharitis, or allergic component. Screen household members for asymptomatic carriage (especially Staph aureus).",
    pearls: "Simple bacterial conjunctivitis is self-limiting (7-10 days without treatment) but topical antibiotics reduce duration to 2-5 days and significantly reduce contagion. Chloramphenicol is widely available and affordable in Kenya. Fusidic Acid has better compliance (BID dosing vs QID) and is preferred for young children. The key differential is viral conjunctivitis — more watery discharge, preauricular lymphadenopathy, and often follows an upper respiratory infection. NEVER patch an infected eye — occlusion promotes bacterial growth. Contact lens wearers are at risk for Pseudomonas keratitis, a sight-threatening emergency requiring urgent ophthalmology review and fluoroquinolone therapy. In Kenya, trachoma (Chlamydia trachomatis) should be considered in chronic cases, especially in rural areas with limited sanitation.",
